# PDFDadi environment variables.
# Copy to .env.local for local development, or set these in your deployment
# environment. See SERVER_SETUP.md for full guidance.
#
# ============================================================================
# PRODUCTION CONFIGURATION GATE
# ============================================================================
# With NODE_ENV=production, PDFDadi validates this file's REQUIRED values at
# process start and REFUSES TO START if any is missing or unsafe. It prints every
# problem at once and exits non-zero, so a bad deploy dies visibly instead of
# booting "healthy" and failing per request. The gate lives in
# src/infrastructure/config/env.ts (`productionProblems`) and is invoked by
# instrumentation.ts.
#
# `next build` is exempt: a build is not a deployment, so CI compiles without any
# production secret.
#
# Everything under an "Optional" heading below is genuinely optional — absent
# billing, R2 or Redis config disables that feature and NEVER blocks startup.
# ============================================================================

# ---- Required in production -------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED in production. An ABSOLUTE SQLite file path on a persistent volume.
# `prisma/schema.prisma` declares provider = "sqlite", and a Prisma datasource
# accepts only its own provider's URLs — it rejects a postgresql:// value when it
# CONNECTS, not at startup, so the deployment would boot healthy and then fail
# every request that reads the database. The production gate refuses anything but
# an absolute file: URL for that reason. Moving to PostgreSQL is a schema-provider
# change plus a regenerated migration history, not a change to this line.
# There is no production default on purpose: a relative path resolves against the
# working directory, which in a container is the image's writable layer, so the
# live database would be deleted by the next deploy. In development, leaving this
# unset uses file:./prisma/dev.db so the app runs with no external services.
#   file:/app/data/db/pdfdadi.db
DATABASE_URL=

# REQUIRED in every environment; at least 16 characters in production. HMAC secret
# that signs admin session cookies AND — unless STORAGE_SIGNING_SECRET overrides
# it — the local storage download URLs. Without it both signatures are forgeable:
# admin takeover with no password and no rate limit, plus arbitrary reads of any
# stored file. Generate a long random value:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# The gate also refuses the public dev fallback string that ships in this repo.
ADMIN_SECRET=

# REQUIRED in production. The public origin, used for SEO/canonical/Open Graph AND
# to build signed download and multipart-upload URLs — so a loopback value here
# hands clients links that resolve to their own machine. The gate refuses
# localhost, 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0 and ::1 in production.
# Left unset it defaults to http://localhost:3000, which is correct for `next dev`
# and refused in production — so a copied-and-forgotten file fails at boot with a
# clear message rather than serving links to the visitor's own machine.
#
# BUILD-TIME **as well as** runtime, and the only variable here that is both.
# `next.config.mjs` bakes the CSP report-to endpoint for `/_next/static/*` from it,
# and the proxy deliberately does not run for those paths, so a runtime value
# cannot supply it afterwards. Pass it to the image build too — the Dockerfile
# takes it as `--build-arg NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=…`, which docker-compose.yml
# already does from this same value.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://pdfdadi.com

# REQUIRED in production, and "single-instance" is the only accepted value.
#
# This is a declaration, not a tuning knob: it makes the operator state the
# topology the rest of the build already assumes. Two things depend on it.
#
#  1. The upload rate limits below are counted in ONE PROCESS's memory
#     (lib/server/uploadRateLimit.ts holds three Maps). Behind a load balancer,
#     N instances each admit the full budget, so the global ceiling — the one
#     bucket a caller cannot rotate away from — becomes N x 240/min.
#  2. `prisma/schema.prisma` declares provider = "sqlite", and SQLite supports
#     ONE writable deployment per database file
#     (docs/adr/ADR-M7-009-sqlite-operations.md). A second writer is unsupported
#     regardless of rate limiting.
#
# There is deliberately no multi-instance value: it would be a lie until there is
# a shared rate-limit store and PostgreSQL. Scaling out is those two changes plus
# this variable, in that order.
DEPLOYMENT_TOPOLOGY=single-instance

# Optional: pre-parse upload abuse control, per minute, per single instance.
# Counted before the multipart body is read, so a refusal costs no parsing.
#   UPLOAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN         120  keyed by authenticated user id
#   UPLOAD_ANON_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN     20  keyed by client address
#   UPLOAD_GLOBAL_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN  240  the ceiling rotation cannot escape
# UPLOAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=120
# UPLOAD_ANON_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=20
# UPLOAD_GLOBAL_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=240

# Optional: shared secret your reverse proxy sends in `x-pdfdadi-proxy-secret`,
# at least 16 characters. Without it `X-Forwarded-For` is NOT read at all: callers
# are keyed by the connection's peer address, which `ingress/guard.mjs` stamps and
# a client cannot set. A forwarding header anyone can send is a rate-limit key
# anyone can rotate, so it counts only when the proxy proves it sent it.
#
# SET IT IF YOU RUN A PROXY. Every request then arrives from the proxy's own
# address, so without the secret all callers share one key and one of them can
# spend the credential endpoints' budget for everyone. The server warns once per
# process when it sees `X-Forwarded-For` with no secret configured.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_SECRET=

# Optional. HMAC key for the analytics subject pseudonym — the daily-rotated
# hash that lets the funnel tell "one visitor, five steps" from "five visitors,
# one step each". Server-only: it is read in the DI container and never reaches a
# client bundle (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and prefixing it would publish it).
#   - Unset: falls back to ADMIN_SECRET, which is required anyway, so funnels
#     work out of the box.
#   - Neither set: analytics still records every event, just unstitched. It is
#     deliberately absent from the validated env schema so a missing value
#     degrades reporting instead of refusing to boot.
# Set it separately when analytics and admin auth should not share a key — e.g.
# so rotating one does not invalidate the other.
# ANALYTICS_SUBJECT_SECRET=

# Optional: usage limit mode — "off", "observe" (default) or "enforce". Observe
# records consumption and never refuses work; enforce turns the plan ceilings in
# src/domain/metering/plans.ts into an actual limit.
# USAGE_LIMIT_MODE=observe

# Optional: public tool API tuning.
# TOOLS_MAX_CONCURRENCY=4
# TOOLS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=20
# TOOLS_MAX_BODY_BYTES=115343360

# Optional: local-dev escape hatch ONLY. Set to "1" to allow the insecure
# public fallback ADMIN_SECRET during `next dev` (admin cookies become
# forgeable — use only on your own machine). Setting it in production is not
# merely discouraged: the production gate refuses to start with it set to "1".
# PDFDADI_ALLOW_INSECURE_DEV_SECRET=0

# ---- Billing (Stripe) -------------------------------------------------------
# All optional. With none of them set, billing is disabled: /api/billing/checkout
# and /api/billing/portal answer 503 BILLING_NOT_CONFIGURED, and every account
# keeps the free plan. There is no partial mode — a key without a webhook secret
# could take money it can never confirm, and a key without a price has nothing to
# sell, so billing switches on only when a key, the webhook secret and at least
# one price are all present.
#
# Every variable below is SERVER-ONLY. None has a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and none
# may be given one: Next.js inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_ values into the client bundle,
# so prefixing either secret publishes it to every visitor. The secret key can
# then move money and read every customer; the webhook secret lets anyone forge a
# "subscription active" event and grant themselves Pro.
#
# Secret API key (sk_test_… in test mode, sk_live_… in production).
#   Stripe dashboard → Developers → API keys.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
#
# Signing secret for the webhook endpoint (whsec_…). Get it when you create the
# endpoint pointing at https://your-host/api/billing/webhook, or from
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/billing/webhook` locally.
# Paid entitlement changes ONLY through signature-verified webhooks — never from
# a checkout redirect — so without this value no subscription is ever applied.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# Price id (price_…, NOT a product id) for Pro — the only purchasable plan. The
# browser never sends a price: a checkout request names "pro" and the id is looked
# up here, and an unmapped price arriving on a webhook grants no paid plan. Left
# unset, Pro is not purchasable and billing stays disabled.
#
# There is no STRIPE_PRICE_BUSINESS. Business billing is deferred: it is still a
# real plan with real entitlements that an operator can grant by hand, but it has
# no price slot, so no Stripe payload can map to it. Setting the variable would
# do nothing.
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=

# ---- Object storage (Cloudflare R2) — optional -------------------------------
# Unset: PDFDadi stores uploads on the local filesystem under STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT.
#
# ALL FOUR OR NONE. A half-configured set silently falls back to local disk, which
# in production means uploads land on an ephemeral container disk and vanish on
# redeploy — data loss presenting as a config typo. The gate refuses the ambiguous
# state and names the missing variables.
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=
#
# Optional public CDN base for public-read objects; omit for private-only.
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# REQUIRED in production **unless** all four R2 variables above are set, and then
# it must be an ABSOLUTE path on a persistent volume. `LocalFileStorage` does
# `path.resolve()` on it, so the `.storage/local` default — right for `next dev` —
# resolves against the working directory: inside a container that is the image's
# writable layer, so every uploaded and generated document would be deleted by the
# next deploy. The production gate refuses a relative or empty value for the same
# reason it refuses a relative DATABASE_URL. docker-compose.yml already sets
#   STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=/app/data/storage
# onto the `pdfdadi-storage` volume; a deployment that is not that compose file has
# to set it itself.
# STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=/app/data/storage

# REQUIRED in production **unless** the working directory is already a persistent
# absolute path outside the build output — which in practice means the shipped
# container and nothing else. Absolute path to the directory holding the admin
# store: `store.json` (all CMS content **and** the operator-set admin password
# hash) plus its `.bak`.
#
# Unset it resolves to `<working directory>/data/admin`, and in production the
# working directory is the BUILD OUTPUT: `.next/standalone/server.js` chdirs into
# its own directory before the app loads, so `npm run start`, systemd and PaaS
# deployments would write the admin password into `.next/standalone/data/admin/` and
# lose it on the next build. That is worse than losing content: with no hash,
# `/admin/setup` re-opens to whoever reaches it first (rate-limited 10/min, no other
# barrier). The production gate refuses a relative value or one inside a `.next`
# directory for that reason.
# docker-compose.yml sets it to /app/data/admin on the `pdfdadi-data` volume.
#   ADMIN_STORE_DIR=/app/data/admin

# Optional: HMAC key for signing local download URLs. Unset, it falls back to
# ADMIN_SECRET. Set it separately when storage URLs and admin auth should not
# share a key. If set in production it must be at least 16 characters — the gate
# refuses a short one rather than letting a placeholder sign real URLs.
# STORAGE_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ---- Background job queue — optional ----------------------------------------
# Unset: the in-memory queue adapter is used, so everything runs with no Redis.
# Set it to move job dispatch out of the server process — e.g. to run
# `npm run worker` as a separate processing container against the same queue.
#
# It does NOT make the app multi-instance. DEPLOYMENT_TOPOLOGY above stays
# single-instance either way: the upload rate limiter is still per-process and the
# database is still single-writer SQLite. A shared queue is one of the three
# things multi-instance would need, not a substitute for the other two.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ---- Set by the platform, not by this file ------------------------------------
# Listed because an operator has to know they exist and what reads them, not
# because they belong in a copied .env. `envContract.test.ts` requires every
# variable the application reads to appear in this file, so an undocumented
# reader fails the suite rather than surfacing on someone's first deploy.
#
# NODE_ENV        BUILD-TIME **and** RUNTIME. `production` is what arms the
#                 configuration gate above, the Secure cookie flag
#                 (lib/admin/session.ts, src/application/services/authHttp.ts),
#                 HSTS (next.config.mjs) and the non-dev CSP. `next build` sets
#                 it to production too and is exempt from the gate via
#                 NEXT_PHASE=phase-production-build.
# PORT            RUNTIME. The port `ingress/server.mjs` binds. Docker sets 3000.
# HOSTNAME        RUNTIME. The interface it binds. Behind a reverse proxy set
#                 127.0.0.1 so the origin is not reachable except through the
#                 proxy; the container publishes a port instead and relies on the
#                 network for that isolation.
# NEXT_PHASE      Set by Next itself. Only read to exempt a build from the gate.
# NEXT_RUNTIME    Set by Next itself. Read so the boot gate runs once, on Node.
# NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED  BUILD-TIME. The Dockerfile sets 1.
# VIPS_CONCURRENCY BUILD-TIME. scripts/next-build.js pins 1 — see its header.

# ---- Optional, with safe defaults ---------------------------------------------

# Optional: server log verbosity — "debug", "info" (default), "warn" or "error".
# NOT free-form: it is a validated enum, so an unrecognised value (LOG_LEVEL=verbose)
# fails the whole configuration parse and the process REFUSES TO START. That is the
# intended behaviour and it is documented here because the failure otherwise reads
# as an unexplained boot loop.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Optional: unified-processing-pipeline override, and the documented rollback
# lever. "off"/"0"/"false" forces the legacy path, "on"/"1"/"true" forces the
# pipeline, unset defers to the database feature flag (default: disabled). It
# applies to the pilot tool only (compress-pdf); no value sweeps the other tools
# in. Env beats the database on purpose: a flag row is no help when the database
# is the thing you are rolling back from.
# PROCESSING_PIPELINE=off

# ---- Processing limits — optional, with safe defaults --------------------------
# All RUNTIME, all read once at module load, all guarded: a blank, non-numeric or
# non-positive value falls back to the default rather than becoming NaN. That guard
# is not cosmetic — every one of these numbers is handed to `setTimeout` or a
# deadline comparison, where NaN silently means "immediately".

# Optional: max pages pdf-to-images will rasterize (default 200). Bounds worst-case
# time, temp disk and zip size; over the limit the caller is told to split the PDF.
# PDF_TO_IMAGES_MAX_PAGES=200

# Optional: execution ceiling for the pipeline compress processor, milliseconds
# (default 180000). Ghostscript on a large scanned PDF is genuinely slow, so this
# is generous on purpose. Raising it above WORKER_STALE_JOB_AFTER_MS below would let
# the reaper reclaim jobs whose worker is alive and still working.
# PROCESSING_COMPRESS_TIMEOUT_MS=180000

# Optional: how often the worker expires jobs past their retention window,
# milliseconds (default 300000).
# PROCESSING_EXPIRY_SWEEP_MS=300000

# ---- Background worker — optional, with safe defaults -------------------------
# Read by `npm run worker` (src/workers/processingWorker.ts). With REDIS_URL unset
# the in-process queue runs inside the server, so these still apply.

# Optional: jobs processed at once (default 2). Floored to at least 1. Each job can
# run Ghostscript or LibreOffice, so this multiplies real CPU and memory, not just
# concurrency.
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2

# Optional: how long shutdown waits for in-flight jobs to drain before killing
# them, milliseconds (default 20000). Keep it below your orchestrator's stop
# timeout, or the platform SIGKILLs the process before the drain finishes.
# WORKER_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=20000

# Optional: how long a running job may go without a write before it is presumed
# abandoned and requeued, milliseconds (default 600000). MUST stay comfortably
# above the longest processor ceiling — PROCESSING_COMPRESS_TIMEOUT_MS above — since
# a processor that works for minutes without reporting a stage refreshes nothing
# while it runs.
# WORKER_STALE_JOB_AFTER_MS=600000

# Optional: how often the stale-job reaper sweeps, milliseconds (default 60000).
# Independent of the threshold above.
# WORKER_STALE_JOB_SWEEP_MS=60000

# Optional: TEST-ONLY. "1" stops the worker module from starting itself on import,
# so a test can drive it by hand. Never set it in production: the queue would fill
# and nothing would drain it.
# WORKER_NO_AUTOSTART=0
